WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.560
Before we start, I will tell you right away
to hold on to your papers.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.549
When I’ve first seen the results I didn’t
do that and almost fell out of the chair.

00:00:13.549 --> 00:00:18.989
Scientists at NVIDIA published an amazing
work not so long ago that was able to dream

00:00:18.989 --> 00:00:22.860
up high-resolution images of imaginary celebrities.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.029
It was a progressive technique, which means
that it started out with a low-fidelity image

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:34.850
and kept refining it, and over time, we found
ourselves with high-quality images of people

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.790
that don’t exist.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:42.070
We also discussed in the previous episode
that the algorithm is able to learn the properties

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:47.240
and features of a human face and come up with
truly novel human beings.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:52.210
There is true learning happening here, not
just copying the training set for these neural

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:53.260
networks.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.489
This is an absolutely stellar research work,
and, for a moment, let’s imagine that we

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:05.239
are the art directors of a movie or a computer
game and we require that the algorithm synthesizes

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.170
more human faces for us.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:12.000
Whenever I worked with artists in the industry,
I’ve learned that what artists often look

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.060
for beyond realism, is ... control.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:21.990
Artists seek to conjure up new worlds, and
those new worlds require consistency and artistic

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.870
direction to suspend our disbelief.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:30.600
So here’s a new piece of work from NVIDIA
with some killer new features to address this.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.450
Killer feature number one.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.560
It can combine different aspects of these
images.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.130
Let’s have a look at an example over here.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:44.710
The images above are the inputs, and we can
lock in several aspects of these images, for

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:48.780
instance, like gender, age, pose and more.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:54.360
Then, we take a different image, this will
be the other source image, and the output

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.690
is these two images fused together.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:02.570
Almost like style transfer or feature transfer
for human faces.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:08.689
As a result, we are able to generate high-fidelity
images of human faces that are incredibly

00:02:08.689 --> 00:02:13.249
lifelike, and, of course, none of these faces
are real.

00:02:13.249 --> 00:02:15.579
How cool is that?

00:02:15.579 --> 00:02:17.150
Absolutely amazing.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.079
Killer feature number two.

00:02:19.079 --> 00:02:24.159
We can also vary these parameters one by one,
and this way, we have a more fine-grained

00:02:24.159 --> 00:02:34.980
artistic control over the outputs.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:41.150
Killer feature number three: It can also perform
interpolation, which means that we have desirable

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:46.599
images A and B, and this would create intermediate
images between them.

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:51.819
As always, with this, the holy grail problem
is that each of the intermediate images have

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:55.540
to make sense and be realistic.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.319
And just look at this.

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:04.041
It can morph one gender into the other, blend
hairstyles, colors, and in the meantime, the

00:03:04.041 --> 00:03:07.439
facial features remain crisp and realistic.

00:03:07.439 --> 00:03:11.459
I am out of words, this is absolutely incredible.

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:18.049
It kind of works on other datasets, for instance,
cars, bedrooms, and of course, you guessed

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:21.269
it right, … cats.

00:03:21.269 --> 00:03:29.569
Now, interestingly, it also varies the background
behind the characters, which is a hallmark

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:31.620
of latent-space based techniques.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.330
I wonder if and how this will be solved over
time.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:40.279
We also published a paper not so long ago
that was about using learning algorithms to

00:03:40.279 --> 00:03:45.260
synthesize, not human faces, but photorealistic
materials.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.529
We introduced a neural renderer that was able
to perform a specialized version of a light

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:53.730
transport simulation in real time as well.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:59.659
However, in the paper, we noted that the resolution
of the output images is limited by the on-board

00:03:59.659 --> 00:04:04.920
video memory on the graphics card that is
being used and should improve over time as

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.199
new graphics cards are developed with more
memory.

00:04:08.199 --> 00:04:14.049
And get this, a few days ago, folks at NVIDIA
reached out and said that they just released

00:04:14.049 --> 00:04:20.790
an amazing new graphics card, the TITAN RTX
which has a ton of onboard memory, and would

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:22.950
be happy to send over one of those.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:24.770
Now we can improve our work further.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:29.511
A huge thank you for them for being so thoughtful,
and therefore, this episode has been been

00:04:29.511 --> 00:04:31.810
kindly sponsored by NVIDIA.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:35.530
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

